      title: "What Is Religion?",
      body: `## A hard word to define

Everyone thinks they know what religion is, but scholars have found it remarkably difficult to define. Any definition must include traditions as different as Christianity, which centers on a personal God; Theravada Buddhism, which does not teach a creator God; Confucianism, which some do not consider a religion at all; and the traditions of indigenous peoples, which may have no word for "religion" because it is not separate from the rest of life. Definitions tend to fall into two groups.

## Substantive definitions

**Substantive** definitions say what religion **is about**: what kind of object or belief makes something religious. The English anthropologist **Edward Burnett Tylor** (1832–1917), in *Primitive Culture* (1871), offered a "minimum definition of religion" as "the belief in Spiritual Beings." Tylor believed that religion began with **animism**, the belief that natural things have souls, and developed toward monotheism. His definition is simple and broad, but it seems to exclude forms of Buddhism and some philosophical religions.

## Functional definitions

**Functional** definitions say what religion **does**: its role in individual or social life. The French sociologist **Émile Durkheim** (1858–1917), in *The Elementary Forms of the Religious Life* (1912), defined religion in social terms:

> A religion is a unified system of beliefs and practices relative to sacred things, that is to say, things set apart and forbidden—beliefs and practices which unite into one single moral community called a Church, all those who adhere to them.
>
> — Émile Durkheim, *The Elementary Forms of the Religious Life*, trans. Joseph Ward Swain (1915)

For Durkheim, the essential distinction was between the **sacred** and the **profane**, and the function of religion was to bind a community together. He argued, controversially, that what a society worships in its gods is ultimately the society itself.

## Experiential definitions

**William James**, whom we met in PHR 101 and PHR 201, defined religion, for the purposes of his *Varieties of Religious Experience* (1902), as "the feelings, acts, and experiences of individual men in their solitude, so far as they apprehend themselves to stand in relation to whatever they may consider the divine." James's definition emphasizes personal experience over institutions and doctrines. It complements Durkheim's social emphasis.

## Ultimate concern

The German-American theologian **Paul Tillich** (1886–1965) defined faith as "**ultimate concern**": the state of being grasped by something that one takes to be of ultimate importance, which claims one's whole life. On this definition, almost anyone has a religion, since everyone takes something as ultimate—God, nation, success, pleasure. The definition helps explain why secular ideologies, such as nationalism or communism, sometimes behave like religions; but critics argue that it stretches the word too far.

## Cultural systems

The American anthropologist **Clifford Geertz** (1926–2006), in an influential essay of 1966, defined religion as a system of symbols that establishes powerful moods and motivations in people by formulating conceptions of a general order of existence, and clothing these conceptions with such an aura of factuality that the moods and motivations seem uniquely realistic. Geertz's definition stresses that religion is a **way of seeing** the world, embodied in symbols and practices.

## Family resemblance

Some scholars have concluded that no single definition will work, and that "religion" is a **family resemblance** concept, in Wittgenstein's sense, which we met in PHR 101: the things we call religions share overlapping features, as members of a family resemble one another, though no single feature is common to all. This course will use such an approach, attending to the many dimensions that religions share in varying degrees.

> **Pause and consider.** Which definition seems most adequate to you? Does any definition include everything you think of as religion, and exclude everything you do not?`,
