      title: "Max Müller and the Birth of Comparative Religion",
      body: `## A new science

The academic study of religions as a comparative discipline is relatively young. Its founder is usually said to be **Friedrich Max Müller** (1823–1900), a German scholar of Sanskrit who spent his career at Oxford. Müller edited the *Rig Veda*, the oldest Hindu scripture, and argued that religions, like languages, could be studied scientifically, by comparing them and tracing their histories.

## He who knows one

In his *Introduction to the Science of Religion* (1873), Müller adapted a saying of **Goethe** about languages and applied it to religion: "**He who knows one, knows none.**" Just as someone who knows only one language does not really understand what language is, someone who knows only one religion does not understand what religion is. Comparison reveals what is distinctive in each tradition and what is shared.

Müller's saying has been controversial. Believers have objected that one can know one's own religion deeply, as a native speaker knows a language, without knowing others. But the saying captures a truth: comparison sharpens understanding, and the features of one's own tradition often become visible only against the background of others.

## The Sacred Books of the East

Müller's greatest achievement was the editing of ***The Sacred Books of the East***, a series of fifty volumes published by Oxford University Press between 1879 and 1910, containing English translations of the scriptures of Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism, Zoroastrianism, Confucianism, Daoism, and Islam, made by the leading scholars of the day. For the first time, English readers could read the Upanishads, the Dhammapada, the Analects, the Tao Te Ching, and the Qur'an side by side. Many of the translations are now in the public domain, and this course will quote several of them.

## The Parliament of Religions

In 1893, at the World's Columbian Exposition in Chicago, the first **World's Parliament of Religions** brought together representatives of the world's religious traditions for seventeen days. Its most memorable speaker was the young Hindu monk **Swami Vivekananda** (1863–1902), a disciple of the Bengali mystic Ramakrishna, who opened his address with the words "Sisters and Brothers of America" and received a long ovation. Vivekananda presented Hinduism, especially the philosophy of **Vedanta**, as a universal religion that accepted all faiths as true paths to the same goal. He became the first great Hindu teacher in the West. The Buddhist reformer **Anagarika Dharmapala** of Ceylon and the Japanese Zen master **Soyen Shaku** also spoke. The Parliament is often seen as the beginning of formal interfaith dialogue.

## The assumptions of the founders

The early comparativists shared assumptions that later scholars questioned. Many assumed that religions **evolve** from "primitive" to "higher" forms, with Christianity, or a liberal version of it, at the top. Many relied on **texts**, especially ancient scriptures, and paid little attention to how ordinary people actually practiced their religion. Many worked within the context of **European empire**, and their categories—"Hinduism," "Buddhism," "world religions"—reflected the interests of colonial administrators and missionaries as well as scholars.

## "World religions"

The very idea of "world religions" is a product of this period. As the historian **Tomoko Masuzawa** showed in *The Invention of World Religions* (2005), the list of "great" or "world" religions taught in universities was shaped in the nineteenth century by European scholars, who grouped the traditions in particular ways. Some of the names themselves are modern: "Hinduism," as a single name for a vast variety of Indian traditions, came into common use in the early nineteenth century. This does not mean that the traditions are not real, but that the categories by which we organize them have a history.

> **Pause and consider.** Müller said, "He who knows one, knows none." Do you agree? What can comparison reveal that the study of one tradition cannot?`,
