      title: "Eliade: The Sacred and the Profane",
      body: `## A historian of religions

**Mircea Eliade** (1907–1986), born in Romania, studied Sanskrit and yoga in India in the late 1920s, taught in Paris after the Second World War, and from 1957 taught at the University of Chicago, where he helped make the "history of religions" a major academic field. His books, including *Patterns in Comparative Religion* (1949), *The Myth of the Eternal Return* (1949), and ***The Sacred and the Profane*** (1957), offered a sweeping account of religious experience across cultures.

## Hierophany

Eliade's starting point was the distinction between the **sacred** and the **profane**, which he took from Durkheim and Otto but developed in his own way. The sacred, he argued, manifests itself in the world, showing itself as something wholly different from the profane, ordinary world. He called such a manifestation a ***hierophany***, from the Greek for "showing of the sacred." A stone, a tree, a mountain, a river may become a hierophany: it remains a stone, but for the religious person it reveals something more. The burning bush that Moses saw (Exodus 3) is a classic example: an ordinary bush becomes the place where God speaks, and Moses must take off his shoes, for the ground is holy.

## Sacred space

For **religious man**, *homo religiosus*, as Eliade called him, space is not uniform. Some places are sacred, set apart from the surrounding chaos, where the sacred has broken through. Such a place becomes a **center**, an ***axis mundi*** or "axis of the world," connecting heaven, earth, and the underworld. Eliade found the image of the center everywhere: the sacred mountain, the cosmic tree, the pillar, the temple, the city founded on a holy site. Jacob's dream at Bethel, where he saw a ladder reaching to heaven and said, "this is none other but the house of God, and this is the gate of heaven" (Genesis 28:17), expresses it.

## Sacred time

Time, too, is not uniform. Ordinary time flows on, but **sacred time**, the time of festivals and rituals, is a return to the time of origins, *in illo tempore*, "in that time," when the gods or ancestors first acted. By reenacting the myth of origins in ritual, religious people participate again in the creative events. The New Year festivals of many cultures, Eliade argued, reenact the creation of the world, renewing it. Eliade contrasted this **cyclical** view of time, which he called the "**eternal return**," with the **historical** view of time introduced by Judaism and Christianity, in which God acts in unrepeatable events.

## Myth

For Eliade, **myth** does not mean a false story, as in ordinary speech. It means a sacred narrative of origins, which tells how the world, or some part of it, came to be, and which serves as a model for human action. To live according to the myths is to imitate the gods and to live in a world that has meaning.

## The desacralized world

Eliade argued that modern Western people live, for the most part, in a **desacralized** world, in which space and time are homogeneous and nothing is sacred. Yet even the modern nonreligious person, he argued, retains traces of religious behavior: special places such as a birthplace or the site of a first love, festivals like New Year's celebrations, and the myths of popular culture. The religious dimension of human life, on this view, is not easily abolished.

## Critics

Eliade's work has been enormously influential and sharply criticized. Critics, such as the American historian of religion **Jonathan Z. Smith**, argued that Eliade generalized too freely from selected examples, ignored the historical and social contexts of religious practices, and imposed a single pattern on very different traditions. Others questioned his assumption that the sacred is a real dimension of experience rather than a scholarly construction. Eliade's political sympathies in Romania in the 1930s, when he wrote in support of the far-right Legionary movement, have also been the subject of serious criticism. Yet *The Sacred and the Profane* remains a classic, and its categories—hierophany, sacred space, sacred time—are still widely used.

> **Pause and consider.** Eliade argued that even nonreligious modern people treat some places and times as special. Can you think of examples from your own life?`,
