      title: "Christian Theologies of Religions",
      body: `## A theological question

When Christians study other religions, a theological question arises: how should Christians think about other faiths? Is there truth in them? Can their adherents be saved? This question has been debated since the early church, as we saw in PHR 101 when Justin Martyr spoke of the "seeds of the Word" among the Greek philosophers.

## Biblical texts

Christians have appealed to several biblical texts that seem to point in different directions. On one side, Peter declared, "Neither is there salvation in any other: for there is none other name under heaven given among men, whereby we must be saved" (Acts 4:12), and Jesus said, "I am the way, the truth, and the life: no man cometh unto the Father, but by me" (John 14:6). On the other side, God "will have all men to be saved, and to come unto the knowledge of the truth" (1 Timothy 2:4); Peter recognized that "in every nation he that feareth him, and worketh righteousness, is accepted with him" (Acts 10:35); and Paul told the Athenians that God "hath made of one blood all nations of men … that they should seek the Lord, if haply they might feel after him, and find him, though he be not far from every one of us" (Acts 17:26–27).

## Three positions

In *Christians and Religious Pluralism* (1983), the British theologian **Alan Race** proposed a typology that has been widely used, though also criticized:

- **Exclusivism** holds that salvation comes only through explicit faith in Christ, and that other religions, whatever truths they contain, are not paths to salvation. This view has been held by many Protestants and, in different forms, by much of the Catholic tradition before the twentieth century, expressed in the formula "outside the church there is no salvation." The Dutch missiologist **Hendrik Kraemer** defended a version of it at the missionary conference of Tambaram in 1938.
- **Inclusivism** holds that salvation comes only through Christ, but that Christ's grace may reach people within other religions, who may be saved without explicit knowledge of him. The German Jesuit **Karl Rahner** (1904–1984) spoke of "**anonymous Christians**": people who, through their response to God's grace in conscience and in their own traditions, are implicitly oriented to Christ.
- **Pluralism** holds that the great religions are equally valid responses to a single divine reality, and that none is uniquely true. The British philosopher **John Hick** (1922–2012), whom we met in PHR 201, called for a "**Copernican revolution**" in theology, in *God and the Universe of Faiths* (1973): just as Copernicus replaced the earth with the sun at the center of the solar system, theology should place God, not Christianity, at the center of the universe of faiths.

## Vatican II

The **Second Vatican Council**, in its declaration ***Nostra Aetate*** (1965), set out the Catholic Church's attitude toward non-Christian religions. It declared that the Church "rejects nothing that is true and holy in these religions," and that they "often reflect a ray of that Truth which enlightens all men," while affirming that the Church proclaims Christ, "the way, the truth, and the life." It spoke with respect of Hinduism, Buddhism, and Islam, and, in a passage of great historical importance after the Holocaust, of the Jewish people, rejecting the charge that Jews as a people were responsible for the death of Christ.

## Evangelical voices

Evangelical theologians have also engaged the question. The British missionary bishop **Lesslie Newbigin** (1909–1998), who served for decades in India, argued in *The Gospel in a Pluralist Society* (1989) that Christians should be neither exclusivist in a narrow sense nor pluralist, but should bear witness to Christ as the clue to history, with humility and openness, leaving the question of others' salvation to God. Other evangelicals have defended forms of exclusivism or inclusivism.

## Other traditions' views

The question is not only a Christian one. Each tradition has its own way of understanding the others. Many Hindus, following Vivekananda, regard all religions as paths to the same goal, though this is itself a particular Hindu view. Islam regards Jews and Christians as "People of the Book," who received genuine revelations, but holds that the Qur'an is the final and complete revelation. Buddhism has generally been tolerant of other practices, while teaching that liberation comes through the Dharma. We will return to these views in later lessons.

> **Pause and consider.** Which of the three positions—exclusivism, inclusivism, or pluralism—seems to you most consistent with the biblical texts quoted above? Are there other options?`,
